description:
Make intentionally paired side-by-side cards in the Cards gallery stretch to equal heights.

Observed issue:

* In the current right-side local two-column group, paired cards such as "This week" and "Recent activity" can end up with different card heights.
* This creates a visually uneven row even though the two cards are intentionally presented as a pair.

Owner direction:

* Paired cards inside an intentional local two-column gallery group should match heights.
* The shorter card should stretch to the height of the taller card in that local group.
* Apply this only to paired/local side-by-side groups.
* Do not introduce global equal-height behavior across the overall gallery.

Preferred implementation:

* Use normal CSS Grid/Flex stretch behavior where possible.
* Keep the local two-column group on stretch alignment.
* Ensure the card/composition wrapper can fill the available track height, for example with h-full, min-h-full, or equivalent only where needed.
* If a card root is a flex column, allow internal content to remain naturally laid out while the card surface itself fills the paired height.

Preserve:

* natural heights for vertically stacked cards elsewhere;
* current container-query behavior;
* current column spans and gallery proportions;
* current card content and interaction;
* no masonry;
* no global row coupling;
* DOM/focus order;
* responsive single-column collapse.

Responsive behavior:

* When the local group collapses to one column, cards should return to natural independent heights.
* Equal-height behavior applies only while the pair is actually rendered side-by-side.

Do not:

* force fixed pixel heights;
* add arbitrary min-heights solely to fake equality;
* equalize unrelated cards in other gallery regions;
* change card padding/content;
* change semantic tokens or theme behavior;
* reintroduce the old global-grid equal-row problem.

Acceptance:

* Paired side-by-side cards in the right/local gallery group render at equal height.
* The shorter card stretches to match the taller neighboring card.
* Equal-height behavior disappears when the group collapses to a single column.
* Vertically stacked cards elsewhere retain natural content-driven heights.
* No overflow, clipping, layout, focus-order, or accessibility regression.
* Browser verification confirms matched card heights at representative widths where the pair is two-column.
